{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Coalwood Way (2000) is the second memoir in a series of three, by Homer Hickam, Jr. "The Coalwood Way" is a story of the Rocket Boys and Coalwood.","title":"The Coalwood Way"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Homer calls it an "equal," rather than a sequel because the story happens during the same timeframe as the first book.","title":"The Coalwood Way"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Today, it is one of the most often picked community/library reads in the United States.","title":"The Coalwood Way"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" It is also studied in many school systems around the world.","title":"The Coalwood Way"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The Coalwood Way (2000) is followed by "Sky of Stone" (2002), and preceded by "October Sky" (1998).","title":"The Coalwood Way"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"October Sky (originally published as Rocket Boys) is the first memoir in a series of three, by Homer Hickam, Jr. It is a story of growing up in a mining town, and a boy's pursuit of amateur rocketry in a coal mining town.","title":"October Sky (novel)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It won the W.D. Weatherford Award in 1998, the year of its release.","title":"October Sky (novel)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Today, it is one of the most often picked community/library reads in the United States.","title":"October Sky (novel)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" It is also studied in many school systems around the world.","title":"October Sky (novel)"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" "October Sky" was followed by "The Coalwood Way" (2000) and "Sky of Stone" (2002).","title":"October Sky (novel)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"One For the Morning Glory is a fantasy novel by John Barnes, published 1996.","title":"One for the Morning Glory"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is a fairy tale where the characters know that they are in a fairy tale.","title":"One for the Morning Glory"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The novel has a humorous tone similar to William Goldman's "The Princess Bride" — quite different from Barnes' usual science fiction.","title":"One for the Morning Glory"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" It forms the second part of a three book series whose first and third parts are not yet written.","title":"One for the Morning Glory"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Mahabharata Quest: The Alexander Secret is the second novel by Indian author Christopher C. Doyle and was released on 9 October 2014 by Westland Publishers.","title":"The Mahabharata Quest: The Alexander Secret"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The story follows Vijay and his friends as they embark on a quest to learn about Alexander the Great's secret mission in India while encountering bioterrorism.","title":"The Mahabharata Quest: The Alexander Secret"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Following his first novel, "The Mahabharata Secret", Doyle was signed for a three book series by Westland.","title":"The Mahabharata Quest: The Alexander Secret"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The author researched further on the "Mahabharata" and was also intrigued by Alexander's invasion of India and his sudden retreat.","title":"The Mahabharata Quest: The Alexander Secret"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Doyle combined secrets from the epic and created the story, stating that he consulted Sanskrit scholars and science experts regarding the theories he put in the book.","title":"The Mahabharata Quest: The Alexander Secret"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" "The Mahabharata Quest: The Alexander Secret" was a commercial success and received positive critical reviews, which praised the storytelling and the theme.","title":"The Mahabharata Quest: The Alexander Secret"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Barbara "Bobbie" Lucille Banda (c. 1947 – May 4, 2013) was an American Juaneño tribal elder, activist, and a member of the Juaneño Band of Mission Indians.","title":"Bobbie Banda"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Banda successfully championed efforts to introduction Native American curriculum, including Juaneño language courses, into the public school systems around San Juan Capistrano, California, during the 1970s.","title":"Bobbie Banda"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The curriculum is still taught in California public schools today.","title":"Bobbie Banda"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Sea Beggars is an incomplete book series by Paul Kearney.","title":"The Sea Beggars"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It began with "The Mark of Ran" (2004) and tells the story of Rol Cortishane.","title":"The Sea Beggars"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" It is based almost entirely around ocean-based adventures.","title":"The Sea Beggars"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" A second volume, "This Forsaken Earth" (original working title "The Stars We Sail By") was published in July 2006.","title":"The Sea Beggars"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The series was expected to be four books long and the third had nearly been completed when the series was unexpectedly dropped by Bantam in May 2007.","title":"The Sea Beggars"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" After signing a new contract with Solaris, Paul announced this would now be a three book series and that the last book in the series was due to be published as an omnibus in Autumn 2012.","title":"The Sea Beggars"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" However, due to the US publishers never releasing the rights for the final book, this remains unpublished.","title":"The Sea Beggars"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: Homer Hickam, Jr.
