{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Isabel Martin Lewis (July 11, 1881 – July 31, 1966) was an American astronomer who was the first woman hired by the United States Naval Observatory as assistant astronomer.","title":"Isabel Martin Lewis"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" In 1918, Lewis was elected a member of the American Astronomical Society.","title":"Isabel Martin Lewis"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" She was also a member of the Royal Astronomical Society of Canada and the Astronomical Society of the Pacific.","title":"Isabel Martin Lewis"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The United States Naval Observatory (USNO) is one of the oldest scientific agencies in the United States, with a primary mission to produce Positioning, Navigation and Timing (PNT) for the United States Navy and the United States Department of Defense.","title":"United States Naval Observatory"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Located in Northwest Washington, D.C. at the Northwestern end of Embassy Row, it is one of the pre-1900 astronomical observatories located in an urban area; at the time of its construction, it was far from the light pollution thrown off by the (then-smaller) city center.","title":"United States Naval Observatory"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The USNO operates the "Master Clock", which provides precise time to the GPS satellite constellation run by the United States Air Force.","title":"United States Naval Observatory"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The USNO performs radio VLBI-based positions of quasars with numerous global collaborators, in order to produce Earth Orientation parameters.","title":"United States Naval Observatory"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Bulletin of the American Astronomical Society ("BAAS"; "Bull.","title":"Bulletin of the American Astronomical Society"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Am.","title":"Bulletin of the American Astronomical Society"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Astron.","title":"Bulletin of the American Astronomical Society"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Soc.")","title":"Bulletin of the American Astronomical Society"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" is the journal of record for the American Astronomical Society established in 1969.","title":"Bulletin of the American Astronomical Society"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" It publishes meetings of the society, obituaries of its members, and scholarly articles.","title":"Bulletin of the American Astronomical Society"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" Four issues are published per year that are collected into a single volume.","title":"Bulletin of the American Astronomical Society"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Royal Astronomical Society (RAS) is a learned society that began as the Astronomical Society of London in 1820 to support astronomical research (mainly carried on at the time by 'gentleman astronomers' rather than professionals).","title":"Royal Astronomical Society"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It became the Royal Astronomical Society in 1831 on receiving its Royal Charter from William IV.","title":"Royal Astronomical Society"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" A Supplemental Charter in 1915 opened up the fellowship to women.","title":"Royal Astronomical Society"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" It is the UK adhering organisation to the International Astronomical Union and a member of the Science Council, and encourages and promotes the study of astronomy, solar-system science, geophysics and closely related branches of science.","title":"Royal Astronomical Society"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Meetings are held in Burlington House, in Piccadilly, London and across the United Kingdom (UK).","title":"Royal Astronomical Society"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" They are involved in the production of astronomical journals and periodicals.","title":"Royal Astronomical Society"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" The society has over 3000 members, around a third of whom live outside the UK.","title":"Royal Astronomical Society"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" In addition, those members of the public who have an interest in astronomy and geophysics and wish to support the work of the society may become Friends of the RAS.","title":"Royal Astronomical Society"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Astronomical Journal (often abbreviated "AJ" in scientific papers and references) is a peer-reviewed monthly scientific journal owned by the American Astronomical Society and currently published by IOP Publishing.","title":"The Astronomical Journal"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is one of the premier journals for astronomy in the world.","title":"The Astronomical Journal"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Until 2008, the journal was published by the University of Chicago Press on behalf of the American Astronomical Society.","title":"The Astronomical Journal"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The reasons for the change were given by the society as the desire of the University of Chicago Press to revise its financial arrangement and their plans to change from the particular software that had been developed in-house.","title":"The Astronomical Journal"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The other two publications of the society, the "Astrophysical Journal" and its supplement series, followed in January 2009.","title":"The Astronomical Journal"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Andrew Fraknoi is an astronomy professor at Foothill College and the 2007 California Professor of the Year awarded by the Carnegie Foundation for the Advancement of Teaching and the Council for Advancement and Support of Education.","title":"Andrew Fraknoi"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Fraknoi also won the Astronomical Society of the Pacific's 2007 Richard H. Emmons award, the American Institute of Physics's 2007 Andrew Gemant Award (given for a lifetime of contributions to the intersection of physics and culture), and the American Astronomical Society's 1994 Annenberg Foundation Award (for a lifetime of contributions to astronomy education.)","title":"Andrew Fraknoi"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The International Astronomical Union has named Asteroid 4859 Asteroid Fraknoi to recognize